{"type":"begin","data":{"path":{"text":"publishingperspectives.com/In London, Geetanjali Shree and Daisy Rockwell Win the 2022 International Booker Prize.txt"}}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"publishingperspectives.com/In London, Geetanjali Shree and Daisy Rockwell Win the 2022 International Booker Prize.txt"},"lines":{"text":"title: In London, Geetanjali Shree and Daisy Rockwell Win the 2022 International Booker Prize\n"},"line_number":2,"absolute_offset":4,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"International Booker Prize"},"start":67,"end":93}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"publishingperspectives.com/In London, Geetanjali Shree and Daisy Rockwell Win the 2022 International Booker Prize.txt"},"lines":{"text":"In London, the 2022 International Booker Prize tonight (May 26) has been won by author Geetanjali Shree and translator Daisy Rockwell for their work on Tomb of Sand (Tilted Axis Press, 2021).The moment carries several important firsts, as listed by the Booker Foundation in its media messaging this evening:\n"},"line_number":14,"absolute_offset":472,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"International Booker Prize"},"start":20,"end":46}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"publishingperspectives.com/In London, Geetanjali Shree and Daisy Rockwell Win the 2022 International Booker Prize.txt"},"lines":{"text":"It's of particular importance to note that the winning International Booker Prize comes from an independent press.\n"},"line_number":24,"absolute_offset":1323,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"International Booker Prize"},"start":55,"end":81}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"publishingperspectives.com/In London, Geetanjali Shree and Daisy Rockwell Win the 2022 International Booker Prize.txt"},"lines":{"text":"In a news conference earlier today, International Booker Prize jury chair Frank Wynne credited independent publishers with the lion's share of translated work. \"Nobody is surprised that the majority of books on the long- and shortlist,\" he said, \"are independent publishers. Small independent presses have done enormous work in bringing translation to people.\"\n"},"line_number":26,"absolute_offset":1439,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"International Booker Prize"},"start":36,"end":62}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"publishingperspectives.com/In London, Geetanjali Shree and Daisy Rockwell Win the 2022 International Booker Prize.txt"},"lines":{"text":"The International Booker Prize, of course, is not to be confused with the Booker Prize for Fiction. This one is focused on translation, and its £50,000 prize (US$63,024) is to be split equally between the winning author and translator.\n"},"line_number":34,"absolute_offset":2204,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"International Booker Prize"},"start":4,"end":30}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"publishingperspectives.com/In London, Geetanjali Shree and Daisy Rockwell Win the 2022 International Booker Prize.txt"},"lines":{"text":"The International Booker Prize 2022 Shortlist\n"},"line_number":52,"absolute_offset":4499,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"International Booker Prize"},"start":4,"end":30}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"publishingperspectives.com/In London, Geetanjali Shree and Daisy Rockwell Win the 2022 International Booker Prize.txt"},"lines":{"text":"As you'll recall, Wynne is the first International Booker Prize jury chair who is a translator. He was joined on the panel by:\n"},"line_number":68,"absolute_offset":5210,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"International Booker Prize"},"start":37,"end":63}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"publishingperspectives.com/In London, Geetanjali Shree and Daisy Rockwell Win the 2022 International Booker Prize.txt"},"lines":{"text":"Geetanjali Shree and Daisy Rockwell, who were seen on Sunday (May 22) at London's Southbank Centre in an afternoon of International Booker Prize shortlistees' readings, are to make their first appearances as winners of the prize at Wales' Hay Festival on May 29.\n"},"line_number":92,"absolute_offset":7089,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"International Booker Prize"},"start":118,"end":144}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"publishingperspectives.com/In London, Geetanjali Shree and Daisy Rockwell Win the 2022 International Booker Prize.txt"},"lines":{"text":"Tonight, the Booker Foundation is reporting that the announcement of the 2021 International Booker Prize-winner, At Night All Blood Is Black, written by David Diop and translated by Anna Moschovakis, saw the book's publisher, Pushkin Press, order a five-figure reprint the day after the winner announcement. The week following the winner announcement, the Booker is reporting, sales of At Night All Blood Is Black saw a 477-percent sale increase over the week before.\n"},"line_number":98,"absolute_offset":8123,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"International Booker Prize"},"start":78,"end":104}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"publishingperspectives.com/In London, Geetanjali Shree and Daisy Rockwell Win the 2022 International Booker Prize.txt"},"lines":{"text":"More from Publishing Perspectives on both Booker Prize programs is here. More on the International Booker Prize is here, more on translation is here, and more from us on international publishing and book awards programs in general is here. More on the Indian market in publishing is here.\n"},"line_number":104,"absolute_offset":8941,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"International Booker Prize"},"start":85,"end":111}]}}
{"type":"end","data":{"path":{"text":"publishingperspectives.com/In London, Geetanjali Shree and Daisy Rockwell Win the 2022 International Booker Prize.txt"},"binary_offset":null,"stats":{"elapsed":{"secs":0,"nanos":91825,"human":"0.000092s"},"searches":1,"searches_with_match":1,"bytes_searched":9329,"bytes_printed":5532,"matched_lines":10,"matches":10}}}
{"type":"begin","data":{"path":{"text":"www.nypl.org/NYPL Recommends World Literature in Translation.txt"}}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"www.nypl.org/NYPL Recommends World Literature in Translation.txt"},"lines":{"text":"Set in Iran in the decade following the 1979 Revolution, this moving, richly imagined novel is narrated by the ghost of Bahar, a 13-year-old girl whose family is compelled to flee their home in Tehran for a new life in a small village, hoping in this way to preserve both their intellectual freedom and their lives. Finalist for the 2020 International Booker Prize.\n"},"line_number":23,"absolute_offset":2256,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"International Booker Prize"},"start":338,"end":364}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"www.nypl.org/NYPL Recommends World Literature in Translation.txt"},"lines":{"text":"On an unnamed island off an unnamed coast, things are disappearing. First, animals and flowers. Then objects—ribbons, bells, photographs. Then body parts. Most of the island's inhabitants fail to notice these changes, while those few imbued with the power to recall the lost objects live in fear of the mysterious \"memory police,\" who are committed to ensuring that the disappeared remain forgotten. When a young novelist realizes that more than her career is in danger, she hides her editor beneath her floorboards, and together, as fear and loss close in around them, they cling to literature as the last way of preserving the past. Finalist for the International Booker Prize and the National Book Award.\n"},"line_number":71,"absolute_offset":8935,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"International Booker Prize"},"start":654,"end":680}]}}
{"type":"end","data":{"path":{"text":"www.nypl.org/NYPL Recommends World Literature in Translation.txt"},"binary_offset":null,"stats":{"elapsed":{"secs":0,"nanos":55920,"human":"0.000056s"},"searches":1,"searches_with_match":1,"bytes_searched":14349,"bytes_printed":1697,"matched_lines":2,"matches":2}}}
{"type":"begin","data":{"path":{"text":"guides.loc.gov/Award-Winning Authors in English Translation.txt"}}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"guides.loc.gov/Award-Winning Authors in English Translation.txt"},"lines":{"text":"- The War of the Poor by Eric Vuillard; Mark Polizzotti (Translator)Call Number: BX4946.M8 V8513 2020ISBN: 9781635420081Published/Created: 2020International Booker Prize Finalist The Spectator (UK): Best Book of the Year. From the award-winning author of The Order of the Day, a powerful account of the German Peasants' War (1524-25) that shows striking parallels to class conflicts of our time. In the sixteenth century, the Protestant Reformation launched an attack on privilege and the Catholic Church, but it rapidly became an established, bourgeois authority itself. Rural laborers and the urban poor, who were still being promised equality in heaven, began to question why they shouldn't have equality here and now on earth. There ensued a furious struggle between the powerful--the comfortable Protestants--and the others, the wretched. They were led by a number of theologians, one of whom has left his mark on history through his determination and sheer energy. His name was Thomas Müntzer, and he set Germany on fire. The War of the Poor recounts his story--that of an insurrection through the Word. In his characteristically bold, cinematic style, Éric Vuillard draws insights from this revolt from nearly five hundred years ago, which remains shockingly relevant to the dire inequalities we face today.\n"},"line_number":90,"absolute_offset":24353,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"International Booker Prize"},"start":143,"end":169}]}}
{"type":"end","data":{"path":{"text":"guides.loc.gov/Award-Winning Authors in English Translation.txt"},"binary_offset":null,"stats":{"elapsed":{"secs":0,"nanos":65730,"human":"0.000066s"},"searches":1,"searches_with_match":1,"bytes_searched":25729,"bytes_printed":1680,"matched_lines":1,"matches":1}}}
{"type":"begin","data":{"path":{"text":"www.englishpen.org/PEN Translates winners announced.txt"}}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"www.englishpen.org/PEN Translates winners announced.txt"},"lines":{"text":"PEN Translates has now supported 376 books translated from over 90 languages, awarding over £1.2m in grants. 19 PEN Translates-supported books have appeared on International Booker Prize longlists. The PEN Translates-supported Kibogo by Scholastique Mukasonga, translated from the French by Mark Polizzotti (Daunt Books), recently won the 2024 Oxford-Weidenfeld Prize. Of Cattle and Men by Ana Paula Maia, translated from the Portuguese by Zoë Perry (Charco Press) – also a PEN Translates award winner – won the 2024 Republic of Consciousness Prize.\n"},"line_number":51,"absolute_offset":3289,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"International Booker Prize"},"start":161,"end":187}]}}
{"type":"end","data":{"path":{"text":"www.englishpen.org/PEN Translates winners announced.txt"},"binary_offset":null,"stats":{"elapsed":{"secs":0,"nanos":52819,"human":"0.000053s"},"searches":1,"searches_with_match":1,"bytes_searched":6561,"bytes_printed":902,"matched_lines":1,"matches":1}}}
