{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"centerforfiction.org/2024 Day of Translation.txt"},"lines":{"text":"Jennifer Croft won a 2022 Guggenheim Fellowship for her novel The Extinction of Irena Rey, the 2020 William Saroyan International Prize for Writing for her illustrated memoir Homesick and the 2018 International Booker Prize for her translation from Polish of Nobel laureate Olga Tokarczuk's Flights. She is also the translator of Federico Falco's A Perfect Cemetery, Romina Paula's August, Pedro Mairal's The Woman from Uruguay, and Olga Tokarczuk's The Books of Jacob (a finalist for the Kirkus Prize). In 2023, she received an American Academy of Arts and Letters Award in Literature. She lives in Tulsa, Oklahoma with her husband and twins\n"},"line_number":77,"absolute_offset":6102,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"International Booker Prize"},"start":197,"end":223}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"centerforfiction.org/2024 Day of Translation.txt"},"lines":{"text":"Elisabeth Jaquette is a translator from Arabic and Executive Director of Words Without Borders. Her translation of Minor Detail by Palestinian author Adania Shibli was shortlisted for the National Book Awards and longlisted for the International Booker Prize. Other translations include Thirteen Months of Sunrise by Sudanese author Rania Mamoun, The Queue by Egyptian author Basma Abdel Aziz, and The Frightened Ones by Syrian author Dima Wannous. Elisabeth's work has appeared or is forthcoming in the New York Times, McSweeney's, Words Without Borders, the Common, and World Literature Today, as well as a dozen anthologies. Formerly, she was Executive Director of the American Literary Translators Association.\n"},"line_number":145,"absolute_offset":10301,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"International Booker Prize"},"start":232,"end":258}]}}
